From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
Date: Tue, 28 Jul 2026 10:14:06 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: ath10k: snoc: the MSA carveout is Device memory, use
 memcpy_fromio()

ath10k_setup_msa_resources() maps the fixed "memory-region" MSA carveout with
devm_memremap(..., MEMREMAP_WT). arm64 defines no ioremap_wt(), so asm-generic
folds MEMREMAP_WT onto plain ioremap(), i.e. PROT_DEVICE_nGnRE. Device memory
does not tolerate the unaligned accesses that arm64's memcpy() emits for the
head and tail of a copy, so ath10k_snoc_dump_msa_region() can take an alignment
fault (ESR FSC 0x21) while dumping the region after a firmware crash -- an oops
in the QMI event workqueue, in the middle of crash handling.

This is latent rather than routinely hit: the function returns early unless a
ramdump buffer has been allocated, so it only fires with coredump ramdumps
enabled. It was found from the write side of the same mapping, where arm64's
__memset() reaches its `dc zva` fast path and faults immediately -- DC ZVA on
Device memory is architecturally an alignment fault.

memcpy_fromio() is correct for both cases: it is required for the memremap()
path, and it is merely a slower-but-valid byte/word copy for the
dmam_alloc_coherent() fallback, which is ordinary memory.

Found on Google Pixel 2 XL (msm8998/wahoo), which takes the fixed-carveout
path because its WLAN firmware rejects a dynamically allocated MSA.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
---
 drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath10k/snoc.c | 15 +++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath10k/snoc.c b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath10k/snoc.c
index 310650227578..8c3871692800 100644
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath10k/snoc.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath10k/snoc.c
@@ -6,6 +6,7 @@
 
 #include <linux/bits.h>
 #include <linux/clk.h>
+#include <linux/io.h>
 #include <linux/kernel.h>
 #include <linux/module.h>
 #include <linux/of.h>
@@ -1474,12 +1475,22 @@ static void ath10k_msa_dump_memory(struct ath10k *ar,
 	hdr->start = cpu_to_le32((unsigned long)ar->msa.vaddr);
 	hdr->length = cpu_to_le32(ar->msa.mem_size);
 
+	/*
+	 * msa.vaddr may come from devm_memremap(..., MEMREMAP_WT) below. arm64
+	 * has no ioremap_wt(), so asm-generic maps MEMREMAP_WT onto plain
+	 * ioremap() == PROT_DEVICE_nGnRE, and plain memcpy() is not valid on
+	 * Device memory: its unaligned head/tail accesses fault (FSC 0x21).
+	 * memcpy_fromio() is correct for both this and the dmam_alloc_coherent
+	 * fallback, which is ordinary memory.
+	 */
 	if (current_region->len < ar->msa.mem_size) {
-		memcpy(buf, ar->msa.vaddr, current_region->len);
+		memcpy_fromio(buf, (const void __iomem *)ar->msa.vaddr,
+			      current_region->len);
 		ath10k_warn(ar, "msa dump length is less than msa size %x, %x\n",
 			    current_region->len, ar->msa.mem_size);
 	} else {
-		memcpy(buf, ar->msa.vaddr, ar->msa.mem_size);
+		memcpy_fromio(buf, (const void __iomem *)ar->msa.vaddr,
+			      ar->msa.mem_size);
 	}
 }
 
